we have a right triangle with a square  inscribed along its right angle leaving  lengths of 5 and 20. what is the area of  the square here's the textbook solution  let X be the side length of the square  then this upper triangle is similar to  this lower triangle meaning x divided by  20 is equal to 5 divided by X cross  multiplying gives x squared is equal to  5 multiplied by 20 which is equal to 100  and that's the area of the square now if  you didn't remember similar triangles  here's a fun way to solve the problem  using outside the box thinking construct  the following congruent upper triangle  and extend the sides of the square to  construct this green rectangle now  clearly these two upper triangles have  the same area and clearly these two  lower triangles have the same area  meaning the square has exactly the same  area as the green rectangle but the  green rectangle has dimensions of 5 and  20 meaning the squares area is equal to  20 times 5 which is equal to one hundred  amazing
 

